```

3. **Call this endpoint** when the user navigates away or closes the tab. In your client code, use the `beforeunload` or `visibilitychange` events to trigger it:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const handleBeforeUnload = () => {
    if (status === 'streaming') {
      fetch(`/api/chat/${chatId}/stop`, {
        method: 'POST',
        body: JSON.stringify({ partialMessage: currentPartialMessage }),
      });
    }
  };

  window.addEventListener('beforeunload', handleBeforeUnload);
  return () => window.removeEventListener('beforeunload', handleBeforeUnload);
}, [status, chatId, currentPartialMessage]);
```

This ensures the partial response is saved even if the tab closes, while the server cleans up the active stream reference [1]. The `after` function guarantees the persistence persists beyond the HTTP response [3].

